  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-10-30",
    "title": "Haunting the Cepheus Flare",
    "explanation": " Explanation:   Spooky  shapes seem to haunt this dusty expanse, drifting through the night in the royal constellation  Cepheus . Of course,  the shapes  are cosmic dust clouds visible in dimly reflected starlight. Far from  your own neighborhood , they lurk above the plane of the Milky Way at the edge of the  Cepheus Flare  molecular cloud complex some 1,200 light-years away. Over 2 light-years across and brighter than most of the other ghostly apparitions, vdB 141 or Sh2-136 is also known as the  Ghost Nebula . It lies at lower right in the starry field of view. Inside the Ghost Nebula are reddish telltale signs of dense cores collapsing in the early stages  of star formation . With the eerie hue of interstellar dust reflecting bluish light from hot young stars of  NGC 7023, the Iris Nebula  stands out against the dark just left of center.  In the broad telescopic frame , these fertile interstellar dust fields stretch almost six full moons across the sky.   Tomorrow's picture:  night on a spooky planet",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/CepheusFlare_Tondinelli.jpg?w=6000&h=4076&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-30-haunting-the-cepheus-flare/",
    "credit": " Mirko Tondinelli ",
    "alt": "Interstellar dust clouds and dark nebulae in the Cepheus molecular cloud.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-10-30T08:02:02.104171+00:00"
